<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trebbia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Trebia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trebbia is a river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, about 120 km long, that rises in the Ligurian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and flows north to join the Po near Piacenza. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> on its banks in 218 BCE, and in June 1799 a Russian and Austrian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-suvorov">Alexander Suvorov</a> defeated the French under Jacques MacDonald there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trebbia.jpg" alt="Trebbia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographer: Gabri80.; Image scaled, sharpened and tonally adjusted by Ian Spackman 26 August 2006 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valle_Trebbia-(scaled-and-sharpened).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century the duchy was a battlefield of others’ wars: Austrian and Franco-Sardinian armies fought at Parma and Guastalla in 1734, the Austrians defeated the French and Spanish at Piacenza in 1746, and in 1799 Alexander Suvorov defeated the French on the <b>Trebbia</b> nearby.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Second Punic War Hannibal destroyed Roman armies at the <b>Trebia</b>, Lake Trasimene and Cannae in 218–216 BCE, but Rome refused terms, and Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus defeated him at the Battle of Zama in 202 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Military</a></li></ul></section>
